Good — here’s a clear breakdown of what you can (likely) deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and the IRS forms you’ll need. I’ll also include a quick approximate math example so you can see the impact. This is general guidance — for final filing or if anything is unusual, check software or a tax pro.

Summary answers up front
- Likely best to take the standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) for 2023 (standard = $27,700), because your itemizable deductions (charity $1,400) are much smaller than the standard amount.
- You can and should report your marketing income as self-employment income (Schedule C). If the $650 is business expense, deduct it on Schedule C.
- Home office: because you have self-employment income and the office is used exclusively/regularly for business, you can claim a home office deduction. Use Form 8829 (regular method) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft).
- Student loan interest: you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Form 1040 Schedule 1), subject to MAGI limits for MFJ — verify your MAGI to confirm full/partial/zero deduction.
- Interest income: because your total interest (Marcus $1,780 + TD Ameritrade $320 = $2,100) exceeds $1,500, you’ll generally file Schedule B for interest/dividends.

Key details and how to treat each item
1) Wages (W-2)
- Report on Form 1040 (box 1 of your W-2).

2) Self-employment income (marketing, $11,200)
- Report gross and expenses on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- If the $650 is an expense related to that business, deduct it on Schedule C.
- Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 (via Schedule 1).
- You will owe self-employment (SE) tax on net earnings: calculate with Schedule SE. Half of SE tax is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are business expenses for your marketing work (self-employed), deduct on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).

4) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- You qualify only if the space is used exclusively and regularly for business and is your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  - Regular method: allocate business percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95%. Deduct that percentage of allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.). Example for rent only: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 × 17.95% ≈ $4,536.
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700.
- If you use the regular method, use Form 8829 to compute and report the deduction on Schedule C.
- Note: home office deduction is a business expense on Schedule C — it does not require you to itemize. You can claim the home office deduction and still take the standard deduction.

5) Student loan interest ($4,800 paid to FedLoan)
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 for 2023 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1), subject to income phase-outs for MFJ. If your MAGI is below the phase-out threshold, you can take up to $2,500; otherwise it may be reduced or disallowed. You should receive Form 1098-E from your servicer showing interest paid.
- The fact you paid $4,800 does not allow more than the statutory limit.

6) Charitable donations ($1,400, you have receipt)
- Charitable cash gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since $1,400 << standard deduction ($27,700), itemizing is unlikely to be beneficial in your case.

7) Interest from savings & brokerage ($1,780 + $320)
- Report as taxable interest on Form 1040. Because total interest > $1,500, include Schedule B.

8) Forms for estimated tax / withholding
- If you expect to owe tax (including SE tax) and not enough is withheld, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments using Form 1040-ES.

Approximate illustrative calculation (rounded)
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self-employment gross: $11,200
- Business expenses (assume $650): net SE income = $10,550
- Interest income: $2,100
- Subtotal income = 82,350 + 10,550 + 2,100 = $95,000
- Approx SE tax (on 92.35% of net SE income): ~ $1,490 → half deductible ≈ $745
- Student loan interest deduction (max allowed) ≈ $2,500 (subject to MAGI phase-out)
- Approx AGI = 95,000 − 745 − 2,500 = $91,755
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = 27,700 → taxable income ≈ $64,055

Notes: those numbers are illustrative and depend on exact deductible amounts, whether the student loan interest is fully allowed, additional business expenses, and any other adjustments.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (e.g., student loan interest adjustment, and report of Schedule C income).
- Schedule C — profit/loss from your marketing business.
- Schedule SE — self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — expenses for business use of your home (if you use the regular method).
- Schedule B — interest and ordinary dividends (required if > $1,500).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (probably not for you).
- Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — you may receive these from clients; you still report the income even if you don’t receive a 1099.
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement from FedLoan (for the student loan interest deduction).
- Form 1040-ES — for making estimated tax payments (if needed).

State taxes
- Illinois: file IL-1040 for state income tax (Illinois taxes income; start from federal AGI). Chicago has no separate city income tax for wage earners. Check IL rules for any adjustments.

Recordkeeping and next steps
- Gather: W-2, any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, 1098-E, 1099-INT/1099-B, receipts for charitable donations, receipts/invoices and records for business expenses, documentation for home office (sketch, square footage calculation, lease showing rent).
- Decide home office method: regular method will likely give a larger deduction here (regular ≈ $4,536 just for rent vs simplified $700), but regular method requires Form 8829 and bookkeeping of other home expenses.
- If you expect to owe an appreciable tax bill due to SE tax/no withholding, consider quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.
- Consider tax software (it will walk you through Schedules C, SE, 8829 and compute SE tax and estimated payments) or a CPA if you want personalized optimization or have additional complexities.

If you want, I can:
