I can help — first a few clarifying questions so I give accurate, actionable guidance:

1. Is the $4,800 the amount of student-loan interest you actually paid during 2023 (and you received a Form 1098‑E for it)?  
2. Are the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the two marketing clients (self‑employment), or are those employee unreimbursed expenses (expenses you incurred as a W‑2 employee)?  
3. Is the 140 sq ft home office used exclusively for that self‑employment marketing work? (If it’s an employee workspace and you’re a W‑2 employee, it generally isn’t deductible since 2018.)  
4. Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, state/local income or sales taxes paid in 2023, or other big deductible items not listed? (SALT is capped at $10,000 if you itemize.)

Summary of how your items are treated and next steps (based on the info you gave)

- Standard deduction vs. itemize
  - For 2023 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $27,700.  
  - Your itemizable amount as stated (charitable = $1,400) is far below $27,700. Unless you have other large itemizable amounts (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.

- Student loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 of student-loan interest can be deducted “above the line” (i.e., it reduces your AGI) if you meet the income limits. Based on the income numbers you gave (W‑2 $82,350 + self‑employment $11,200 + interest $1,780 + $320 = about $95,650 gross), you are well below typical phase-out thresholds, so you should be eligible for some or all of the $2,500 maximum — but I’ll confirm once you answer Q1 above. The student‑loan interest deduction is claimed on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) of Form 1040.

- Self‑employment income and expenses
  - The $11,200 of marketing income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Business expenses directly related to that work (the $650, if they are business expenses) can be deducted on Schedule C.
  - Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net self‑employment earnings; you’ll compute Schedule SE and pay SE tax (approx. 15.3% on net earnings, with a small adjustment to the taxable base).
  - You may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments if withholding does not cover your income and SE tax.

- Home office
  - You said 140 sq ft exclusive use in a 780 sq ft home → business percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95% (≈18%).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
    - Regular (actual expense) method: multiply business percentage by allowable home expenses. For rent: $2,100/month × 12 = $25,200 annually; 17.95% × $25,200 ≈ $4,528 would be the deductible portion of rent (plus a prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs attributable to the space). If you use the regular method and your net profit from the business is small, home‑office deductions cannot generate a business loss that is disallowed due to passive rules — but if there’s net profit, you generally claim it on Schedule C and use Form 8829 if using the regular method.
  - Important: the home office deduction only applies to self‑employment/business use (Schedule C). It is not available to employees (W‑2) except in very limited categories.

- Charitable contribution
  - Your $1,400 donation is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction ($27,700) will likely be larger than your total itemized amounts, you probably will not claim this unless you have other itemizable items that push you above $27,700.

- Interest income reporting
  - You reported $1,780 (Marcus) + $320 (TD Ameritrade) = $2,100 total interest. Because total taxable interest > $1,500, you will complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) and report the total on Form 1040.

- Forms you will likely need (2023 tax year)
  - Form 1040 (individual income tax return)  
  - Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction appears here if claimed  
  - Schedule B — because your interest income exceeds $1,500  
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $11,200 marketing income and related business expenses  
  - Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax  
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular method for the home office deduction (not needed for simplified method)  
  - Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not)  
  - Possibly Form 8995 or 8995‑A if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Schedule C QBI may allow up to 20% deduction depending on taxable income and other factors) — we can check eligibility once we finalize AGI/taxable income.

Quick illustration (rough example — I’ll refine if you confirm items)
- Gross income ≈ $95,650 (W‑2 + self‑employment + interest).  
- If self‑employment expenses of $650 are legitimate business expenses, net self‑employment income ≈ $10,550.  
- Home office (regular method) could reduce that further (e.g., ≈ $4,528 allocated rent) — but regular method is limited by net profit; you cannot create excessive loss beyond business rules. If you instead use simplified method, take $700.  
- Self‑employment tax and half of that is an above‑the‑line deduction (see Schedule SE).  
- Student loan interest up to $2,500 would reduce AGI.  
- After above-the-line adjustments, you then take the standard deduction $27,700 (unless you have large itemized deductions).

Recommendations / next steps
1. Confirm answers to the 4 clarifying questions above (student‑loan interest amount/1098‑E; whether the $650 is business-related; exclusive use of the home office; any other deductible items).  
2. Decide whether you want to use the simplified home office method ($5/sq ft = $700) or the regular method (may give a larger deduction here because of your rent) — if you expect other home-related prorated expenses (utilities, repairs), the regular method could be better but requires Form 8829 and careful recordkeeping. If you prefer simplicity and you want to avoid complexity, the simplified method is straightforward.  
3. Gather documents: W‑2, Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑K or records for $11,200 self‑employment income, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), bank/ broker 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV, receipts for charitable gifts, records for rent/utilities for home office, and receipts for the $650 expenses (if business).  
4. If you expect similar self‑employment income in future years, consider estimated quarterly payments to avoid penalties.  
5. If you want, I can run approximate numbers (AGI, taxable income, estimated tax) if you confirm the clarifying items above — and tell you whether the regular or simplified home office method looks better in your case.

If you’d like, I can:
- Compute an estimated tax return (AGI, taxable income, rough federal tax + self‑employment tax) with the assumptions you confirm, or  
- Draft a checklist email you can send to your tax preparer with all required items, or  
