Thanks — I can walk through this. I’ll assume you want a clear, practical answer (not formal tax advice). I do need one quick clarification before finalizing numbers: was the $11,200 from “marketing, 2 clients” paid to you as an independent contractor / 1099 (self‑employment), or was it wage income on a W-2? Also, confirm the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses related to that marketing work. If it is self‑employment income, everything below applies; if it was employee wages, the home‑office and unreimbursed expenses treatment is different.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Likely take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) — your itemizable deductions (charitable $1,400) are well below that.
- You can probably claim the student‑loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) as an above‑the‑line adjustment, so it reduces your AGI whether you itemize or not (subject to income limits).
- If the $11,200 is self‑employment income, you can claim a home‑office deduction and the $650 business expenses on Schedule C; that will lower both income and self‑employment tax.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (business use of home) if you use actual expenses, and your 1099/1099‑INT/1099‑B or W‑2s. Also file your Illinois return (IL‑1040).

Detailed breakdown and numbers (based on what you reported)
1) Income (your figures)
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $82,350
- Marketing (2 clients): $11,200 — treated below as self‑employment
- Interest: $1,780 (Marcus) + $320 (TD Ameritrade) = $2,100
Total gross income ≈ $95,650

2) Student loan interest
- You paid $4,800, but the student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 per year (above‑the‑line adjustment).
- The deduction is phased out at higher MAGI; based on your reported income level you are likely under the phaseout and should get the full $2,500. If you want I can compute your exact MAGI and confirm.

3) Charitable contribution
- $1,400 cash gift — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the 2023 MFJ standard deduction is $27,700, $1,400 will not beat the standard deduction by itself.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If those $650 are business expenses related to self‑employment, they’re deductible on Schedule C. If you were an employee and these are unreimbursed employee expenses, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

5) Home‑office (140 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- Eligibility: Allowed if you are self‑employed and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. Not deductible if you are just an employee working from home.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700
  b) Actual expenses method: allocate a share of your rent to the office.
     - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
     - Office percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 0.17949
     - Home‑office deduction (rent portion) ≈ $25,200 × 0.17949 ≈ $4,526
- Because your rent is relatively high, the actual‑expense method produces a much larger deduction (~$4,526 vs $700). Use Form 8829 when using actual expenses. The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus income and self‑employment tax).

6) Self‑employment tax and other adjustments (illustrative)
- If you use the actual home‑office amount and deduct $650 business expenses:
  - Schedule C net ≈ 11,200 − 650 − 4,526 = ≈ $6,024
  - Approximate SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% of net ≈ $851
  - Half of SE tax (≈ $425) is an above‑the‑line deduction
  - Student loan interest deduction (if fully allowed) = $2,500
  - Total above‑the‑line adjustments ≈ $2,925
  - AGI ≈ 95,650 − 2,925 ≈ 92,725
  - Minus standard deduction 27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,025
- If you instead use the simplified home‑office ($700), numbers change only slightly — actual method looks clearly better given your rent.

7) Other items to watch for
- Brokerage/TD Ameritrade: if the $320 is interest/dividend you’ll get a 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV; if there are sales you’ll get a 1099‑B and may owe capital gains tax.
- You may qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI / 199A) deduction (20% of qualified business income) — you may get an additional small deduction on top of Schedule C net, depending on your final taxable income and business type.
- Because you have net self‑employment income, you may owe estimated tax payments (quarterly) for federal and state; self‑employment tax is in addition to income tax.

8) Forms you will likely prepare and attach
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: e.g., student loan interest, half SE tax)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $11,200 and business expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if using actual expenses
  - Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (but likely not beneficial)
  - Form 8995/8995‑A if claiming QBI (depends on your situation)
  - 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / W‑2 / 1099‑INT / 1099‑B as supporting documents
- State (Illinois):
  - IL‑1040 and any required attachments. Illinois has its own rules; you’ll will likely pay IL tax on your taxable income (flat rate).

Recommendation / next steps
1. Confirm: was the $11,200 self‑employment (1099) or W‑2? Are the $650 expenses tied to that work? Do you rent your residence? (You said you pay $2,100/mo, which sounds like rent.)
2. If it’s self‑employment, I recommend using actual home‑office expenses (Form 8829) — it yields the larger deduction here — and claim Schedule C/Schedule SE.
3. Use the standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemizing (your charitable $1,400 won’t beat the standard).
4. Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑B, rent receipts or lease, receipts for charitable gift and student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E), record of the $650 business expenses.
5. If you’d like, I can:
   - Compute a more exact estimated tax (federal and IL) using the confirmed classification of the $11,200 and any other items you haven’t listed.
   - Create a checklist email you can send to your tax preparer (I can draft it).
   - Walk through completing Schedule C / 8829 calculations step by step.

Disclaimer: this is general guidance. For final filing and complex questions (QBI, state specifics, phaseouts), consult a CPA or tax professional.

Tell me whether the $11,200 was self‑employment income and whether you want me to compute an estimated tax bill (I can run the exact numbers and show line‑by‑line calculations).

