Thanks — I can help. Below I’ll cover what you can deduct, whether to itemize vs. take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, estimated tax paperwork you’ll need, and a short checklist of next steps and questions I still need.

Summary (quick)
- Reportable income: W-2 wages ($82,350), self-employment/marketing income ($11,200), interest from Marcus ($1,780), and $320 from TD Ameritrade (report as interest/dividends as shown on year-end statements).
- Likely better to take the 2023 married filing jointly standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemize, given the numbers you gave.
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 of your $4,800 is deductible (subject to MAGI phaseouts — see note).
- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive) looks allowable for your self-employment; using the regular (actual) method will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- You must file Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Schedule B (if interest/dividends total > $1,500), and Form 1040. You may also use Form 8995 for the QBI deduction.

Details and calculations

1) Treatment of each income item
- W-2 from Metro Consulting ($82,350): report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self-employment/marketing ($11,200): report on Schedule C. Business expenses (home office, qualifying business expenses) reduce Schedule C profit. If you received any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from clients include those amounts; even without a 1099 you must report the income.
- Marcus savings interest ($1,780): taxable interest — include on Form 1040. Because your total interest (> $1,500), you will generally need Schedule B.
- TD Ameritrade $320: report as shown on your brokerage year-end statements — could be interest, dividends, or proceeds; follow the 1099 statements from TD Ameritrade. If total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500, Schedule B is required.

2) Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo = $25,200/yr)
- Qualification: Must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). You indicated exclusive use, so it likely qualifies.
- Methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction.
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses. Your allocation = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95%. If you only provided rent, rental portion = $25,200 × 17.95% ≈ $4,523. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., those can be pro-rated and increase the deduction.)
- The actual method will likely give a larger deduction here (≈ $4,523 vs. $700).
- Home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C and reduces your net self-employment profit. Note: for renters you do not take depreciation on the dwelling (depreciation applies to owners, which can have other tax consequences).

3) Student loan interest ($4,800 paid)
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (for 2023); the full $4,800 you paid does not fully qualify.
- There are MAGI phaseouts for the deduction; for most married-filing-joint taxpayers with AGI in the low-to-mid six figures the deduction phases down — based on the income information you gave, you should be eligible for the full $2,500, but the final determination depends on your exact MAGI after adjustments.
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above-the-line” adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) and reduces your AGI directly.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If instead these are business expenses for your self-employed marketing work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against that self-employment income.

5) Self-employment tax and QBI
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net Schedule C profit (after expenses including home office). SE tax rate ~15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings; you can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) Section 199A: you may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income as an additional deduction. With your income scale, you are likely under the threshold where wage/qualified-property limitations become an issue, so a QBI deduction may apply. For example (rough estimate below) you may have a QBI deduction of roughly $1,200 (20% of the estimated net business income after expenses), but exact amount depends on final Schedule C net profit and other taxable income.

6) Itemize vs. Standard deduction — recommendation
- Standard deduction for MFJ (2023): $27,700.
- Your itemizable amounts you listed: charitable donations $1,400. (State and local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc., would also count but you didn’t list any.) Because $1,400 is far below the standard deduction, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest is not an itemized deduction — it’s an adjustment that you can take even if you take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you will generally need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: half self-employment tax deduction, student loan interest, etc.)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required if interest + dividends > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $11,200 and business expenses (home office, business supplies, etc.)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income Deduction) — or 8995-A if you need the more complex computation (but most small businesses use 8995).
- Schedule A only if you itemize (I don’t expect you will).
- State: Illinois IL-1040 (Illinois has a flat tax; include the interest and other income per IL rules).
- Keep any 1099-INT, 1099-NEC, 1099-DIV, 1099-B, and W-2 forms for reporting and matching.

8) Recordkeeping and other notes
- Keep receipts for charitable donations (you have receipt for St. Mary’s — good).
- Keep bank and brokerage 1099s (Marcus, TD Ameritrade).
- Keep records supporting home office (floor plan, square footage, rent, utility bills).
- If you expect to owe extra tax because of self-employment, consider making estimated tax payments quarterly in the current year to avoid underpayment penalties.

Estimated example (rough, illustrative)
- Gross self-employment income: $11,200
- Home office (actual method): ≈ $4,523
- Other business expenses (if the $650 applies to your business): -$650
- Estimated Schedule C net: ≈ $6,027
- Estimated self-employment tax on that net: ≈ $850–$950
- Half SE deduction (above-the-line): ≈ $425–$475
- Student loan interest deduction (above-the-line): up to $2,500 (not the full $4,800)
- Given these numbers, you will very likely take the $27,700 standard deduction rather than itemize.

Questions I still need
1. Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses for your W-2 job (employee) or for your marketing/self-employed activity? (If they’re business expenses for the freelance work, they are deductible on Schedule C; if employee expenses, they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.)
2. Is the $320 from TD Ameritrade interest, dividends, or proceeds from sales? (Look at your 1099 from TD Ameritrade; it will show 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, and/or 1099-B.)
3. Do you pay any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, state/local taxes > $10k limit, unreimbursed medical expenses, property taxes, etc.)?
4. Is your rental payment $2,100/month inclusive of utilities? (If you pay utilities separately, those can be partly allocated to the office portion and increase the home-office deduction.)

Recommended next steps
- Gather W-2, all 1099s (Marcus, TD Ameritrade, any 1099-NEC), receipts for charitable giving, student loan interest statement (Form 1098-E), documentation for home office and business expenses.
- If you want, I can:
  - Walk through a detailed worksheet figure-by-figure and estimate your federal tax liability from the numbers above, or
  - Help draft an email to a tax preparer with the numbers and ask for a professional review, or
  - Save a checklist of documents you need for filing.

If you want more precise numbers (exact AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax and self-employment tax), tell me whether the $650 is a business expense and confirm the TD Ameritrade $320 type and I’ll run the more detailed calculation. Also say if you want me to prepare a document listing the forms and amounts to hand to a tax preparer.

