Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I’ve forwarded your exact question to your CPA (vince2000@gmail.com) as you requested.

Below is a clear, practical summary of what you can deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll generally need. I’ve included an example calculation using the amounts you gave and a couple of clarifying questions at the end.

Key facts you gave (for reference)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (IL)
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self-employment/contract income (marketing, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest income: $1,780 (Marcus savings)
- Dividends / brokerage: $320 (TD Ameritrade)
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year for a 780 sq ft residence
- Home office: 140 sq ft, exclusive use
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (FedLoan)
- Charitable contributions (cash, receipt): $1,400
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $650 (unclear whether these are for self-employment or a W-2 job)

What you can (generally) deduct
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an adjustment to income (above-the-line), subject to income phaseouts. For MFJ in 2023 the phaseout is MAGI $145,000–$175,000. Based on the income you reported, you should be under the phaseout, so you can take the $2,500 maximum (even though you paid $4,800).
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 deductible if you itemize. Not deductible if you take the standard deduction.
- Self-employment business expenses: If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your marketing/self-employment activity, they are deductible on Schedule C. (If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025.)
- Home office: Deductible only if you are self-employed/using the space for an eligible business (Schedule C). Not deductible for W-2 employees. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
  - Actual-expense method: take the business percentage of your eligible home expenses. Business percentage = 140 / 780 ≈ 17.95%. If you use rent only as the eligible expense, the rent portion = $25,200 × 17.95% ≈ $4,530. You may also allocate a percentage of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. The actual method yields a much larger deduction here, but requires records and (for actual method) Form 8829 to calculate the home-office expense and attach to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method you do not file Form 8829.
- Self-employment tax: You will owe self-employment tax on net self-employment income (Schedule SE, roughly 15.3% on net earnings). You can deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an above-the-line adjustment (reduces AGI).

Quick illustrative calculation (with assumptions)
- Total gross income = 82,350 (W-2) + 11,200 (self-employment) + 1,780 + 320 = 95,650.
- Assume your $650 is a business expense (if not, substitute accordingly) and you use the actual home-office method (rent portion only) of ~$4,530.
- Self-employment expenses = $650 + $4,530 = $5,180.
- Net self-employment income = 11,200 − 5,180 = 6,020.
- Self-employment tax base = 6,020 × 92.35% ≈ 5,563 → SE tax ≈ 5,563 × 15.3% ≈ $851.
- One-half SE tax deductible = ~$426 (above-the-line).
- Student loan interest deduction allowed = $2,500 (above-the-line).
- Above-the-line adjustments total ≈ $2,926 (2,500 + 426).
- Estimated AGI ≈ 95,650 − 2,926 = 92,724.
- Itemized deductions available (given info): charitable $1,400 (no mortgage interest, no SALT given) → total ≈ $1,400.
- 2023 standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700.

Conclusion: With the amounts you provided, the standard deduction ($27,700) is much higher than your likely itemized deductions (~$1,400), so you should take the standard deduction. That said, claiming the home-office actual-expense method reduces your self-employment net income and thus reduces taxable income; the home-office deduction itself is applied on Schedule C (it’s not an itemized deduction) and therefore can reduce AGI and tax even while you still take the standard deduction on Form 1040.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — to report adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction shows up here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the marketing/self-employment income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax and the deductible half of SE tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (use this if you take the actual-expense method for home office). If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829; instead report the simplified home-office deduction on Schedule C.
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC / 1099-INT / 1099-DIV — you should have these forms for the incomes you reported (report the amounts even if you didn’t receive a 1099).

Other practical notes and caveats
- If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are for a W-2 job, they are nondeductible under current law. If they are business expenses for your marketing income, include them on Schedule C.
- The home-office deduction requires exclusive and regular use of the space for business and that the space be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.). Keep photos/layout and records showing exclusive use.
- Using the actual home-office method can materially lower your self-employment taxable income (and AGI) but requires documentation (rent receipts, utility bills). The simplified method is easier but smaller.
- You’ll still owe federal income tax based on taxable income and the self-employment tax; if you have little withholding you may need to make estimated tax payments.
- Illinois state return: Illinois will want your federal AGI as the starting point; state rules differ but the federal treatment of self-employment and student loan interest will feed into the IL return.

Quick follow-ups I need to give a tighter estimate or finalize filing recommendation
1. Is the $650 in unreimbursed expenses related to your self-employment/marketing work, or are those expenses for a W-2 job? (If they’re self-employment expenses, include them on Schedule C.)
2. Do you have any other deductions/expenses (state income tax paid, mortgage interest, property tax, medical expenses, other charitable gifts) not listed?
3. Do you prefer the simplified home-office method for simplicity, or do you have records (rent, utilities) and want to use the actual method (which likely gives the bigger deduction here)?

If you’d like, I can:
- Prepare a draft calculation with both home-office methods and show your estimated federal tax and self-employment tax amounts; or
- Draft a list of documents to give your CPA (1099s, receipts, rent statements, student loan interest statement 1098-E, donation receipts, etc.); or
- Draft the text of a message to your CPA summarizing these numbers (I’ve already forwarded your original note to them per your preference).

Which of those would be most helpful next?

